    text why "stall: why?" #b5482e at.data [1984,41] font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Caught up, then fell back"

What happens when a whole country is run from one plan? The Soviet Union tried it for sixty years, and the numbers are in.
    draw axes

    pause 0.3

B: Which numbers? Soviet planners were famous for announcing records.
    highlight axes

Western reconstructions instead: Soviet income per person as a share of America's. In (@draw t28 plan@) 1928, as the first five-year plan began, about a fifth.
    draw line_2

By 1950, after a war that killed some twenty-seven million Soviet citizens, about thirty percent.
    draw t50

B: And by 1975, thirty-eigh (@draw t75@)t. The gap with the richest country on earth was closing.
    animate stage 1 duration 3

In 1961, Paul Samuelson's best-selling textbook projected that the Soviet  (@draw samq@)economy might overtake America's as early as 1984.
    draw sam

    erase sam samq t50

    hide line_2

    ask "Draw what you think happened to Soviet income, as a share of America's, from 1975 to 1989." intro "So did it?" on line_1 from 1975 store g voice b
        right: Close: it fell back.
        wrong: Look at the end of the line: here, it fell back.

It turned down. By 1989, two years before the Soviet Union ended, thirty-one percent and falling.
    draw t89

    pause 0.4

B: Nearly fifty years of catching up, then fifteen of falling behind.
    highlight line_1

So the puzzle comes in two (@draw how@) halves: how did a plan grow so fast,  (@draw why@)and why did it stall?

    pause 0.5

## How the plan worked
vars:
    T:
      value: 100
      min: 50
      max: 200
    ST:
      expr: 3*T
    S:
      expr: 3*T+900
    C:
      expr: 1.5*(3*T+900)
sources:
    - id: hough1979
      title: How the Soviet Union Is Governed
      authors: Jerry F. Hough, Merle Fainsod
      year: 1979
      finding: 'Hough & Fainsod (1979), p. 390, as cited by secondary sources: in 1973 Gosplan worked out material balances for 1,943 of the most important items, about 70% of industrial production. Not checked against the book here, so the lecture says ''about two thousand''.'
    - id: leontief
      title: Input-output economics
      authors: Wassily Leontief
      year: 1966
      finding: 'Each sector''s output is input to others: gross output x = Ax + d, solved as x = (I − A)^-1 d.'

    box gosplan "Gosplan" #3d3833 x 180 y 570 width 250 height 90 radius 10 icon "office building" font_size 30 hidden true
    box mins "Ministries" #3d3833 x 180 y 430 width 250 height 90 radius 10 font_size 30 hidden true
    box facs "Factories" #3d3833 x 180 y 290 width 250 height 90 radius 10 icon factory font_size 30 hidden true
