    arrow a2 from.anchor bottom from.ref mins to.anchor top to.ref facs hidden true
    text tgt "targets: tonnes, pieces" #2f6b8f x 180 y 195 font_size 30 hidden true
    text bal_t "Steel balance (illustrative)" x 680 y 615 font_size 28 hidden true
    box made "Steel made\n{S:0,} kt" #2f6b8f x 530 y 470 width 230 height 110 radius 10 font_size 28 hidden true
    text eq "=" x 672 y 470 font_size 48 hidden true
    box n_tr "tractors {ST:0} kt" #b5482e x 830 y 540 width 260 height 62 radius 10 icon tractor font_size 30 hidden true
    box n_car "cars 600 kt" #3d3833 x 830 y 470 width 260 height 62 radius 10 font_size 30 hidden true
    box n_rail "rails 300 kt" #3d3833 x 830 y 400 width 260 height 62 radius 10 font_size 30 hidden true
    box coal "Coal needed\n{C:0,} kt" #8f887c x 530 y 290 width 230 height 100 radius 10 font_size 28 hidden true
    arrow a3 from.anchor top from.ref coal to.anchor bottom to.ref made hidden true
    text tr_t "Tractor target: {T:0},000" #b5482e x 830 y 300 font_size 28 hidden true
    text cnt "1973: about 2,000 key goods balanced\nmillions of products made" #8a5fa8 cites ["hough1979"] x 640 y 175 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c_save "save: steel, dams, power" #2f6b8f x 680 y 560 font_size 38 hidden true
    text c_farm "farm → factory" #2f6b8f x 680 y 470 font_size 38 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mx work [{"tex":"\\text{each t of steel uses 0.2 t of steel}"},{"tex":"x = d + 0.2x"},{"tex":"x = d \\div 0.8 = 1.25\\,d"},{"tex":"\\text{Leontief: } x = Ax + d"}] x 500 y 380 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "How the plan worked"

The fast half first. How do you run an economy when prices don't do the steering?
    draw gosplan

B: You write it down. At the top, Gosplan, the stat (@draw a1 mins@)e planning committee, set targets f (@draw a2 facs@)or every ministry, and each ministry for its factories.

Targets in quantities: tonnes of steel, metres of cloth, numbers of tractors. Prices were set too, but mostly to keep the books.
    draw tgt

B: Then how did the planners know there would be enough steel for all those tractors?
    draw n_tr tr_t

With a balance for each good: the steel to be made has to match the steel that everyone's targets need.
    draw bal_t made eq n_car n_rail

In 1973 Gosplan itself balanced only about two thousand of the most important goods, in an economy that made millions of different products.
    draw cnt

Try it, with illustrative numbers: raise the tractor target, and watch how much more steel, and then coal, the plan suddenly needs.
    draw coal a3

    erase cnt

    animate T 160 duration 2.5

    explore params ["T"]

B: One target moves, and every balance upstream has to be redone. Largely by hand.
    highlight made coal

    erase bal_t made eq n_tr n_car n_rail coal a3 tr_t

Clumsy. And yet, for forcing growth, a plan is a p (@draw c_save@)owerful tool. It can decide to save: hold consumption down and pour output into steel mills, dams and power stations.

B: And it can move millions of peasants off the farms into factories, where each worker produces far more.
    draw c_farm

That's the fast half: a plan is very good at doing a few big, obvious things, all at once.
    highlight gosplan c_save c_farm

    fade gosplan mins facs a1 a2 tgt c_save c_farm to 0.15

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the arithmetic behind those balances?

@math

    erase mathq

Each tonne of steel takes some steel to make: in the mills, the rails, the machines. Say a fifth of a tonne.
    draw mx_box mx_line_1

So to deliver d tonnes, the plan must make x equals d plus a fifth of x.
    draw mx_line_2

That's d divided by 0.8: a quarter more than the final need.  (@draw mx_line_4@)Wassily Leontief built thousands of such rows into one input–output table.
    draw mx_line_3

    pause 0.5

    erase mx

@test

    erase mathq

    fade gosplan mins facs a1 a2 tgt c_save c_farm to 1

## Where it stalled
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 1928–40
      - 1950s
      - 1960s
      - 1970s
      - 1980s
    values:
      - 3.8
      - 3.3
      - 3.5
      - 1.4
      - 1.1
    value_labels: true
    colors:
      - blue
      - blue
      - blue
      - red
      - red
    y_label: Soviet growth per person, % a year
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 110
      w: 650
      h: 540
    label_size: 30
sources:
    - id: maddison2023
      title: Maddison Project Database 2023, via Our World in Data
      authors: Bolt & van Zanden
      year: 2024
      finding: 'USSR GDP per person, average annual growth: 1928–40 3.8%, 1950–60 3.3%, 1960–70 3.5%, 1970–80 1.4%, 1980–89 1.1% (computed).'
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/gdp-per-capita-maddison-project-database
    - id: weitzman1980
      title: The 'Ratchet Principle' and Performance Incentives
      authors: Martin L. Weitzman
      year: 1980
      finding: When next year's target is based on this year's performance, managers hold back output and hide capacity.
    - id: kornai1980
      title: Economics of Shortage
      authors: János Kornai
      year: 1980
      finding: Shortage is chronic and self-reproducing in the classical socialist economy.
    - id: easterlyfischer1995
      title: The Soviet Economic Decline
      authors: William Easterly, Stanley Fischer
      year: 1995
      finding: The fall in Soviet growth 1950–87 is accounted for by a falling marginal product of capital, given a low elasticity of substitution between capital and labour; TFP growth roughly constant.
      doi: 10.1093/wber/9.3.341
    - id: allen2003
      title: 'Farm to Factory: A Reinterpretation of the Soviet Industrial Revolution'
      authors: Robert C. Allen
      year: 2003
      finding: Soviet growth was strong to about 1970; the slowdown came from diverting resources to the military and wasteful investment, not from planning as such.

    icon boat size 80 x 820 y 560 or ["boat"] icon_look picture of sailboat hidden true
    text by "Harbourtown boatyard" #8f887c x 820 y 490 font_size 30 hidden true
    text tg "target: 20 boats" x 820 y 435 font_size 30 hidden true
    text cb "could build: 30" #2f6b8f x 820 y 385 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c1 "ratchet" #b5482e cites ["weitzman1980"] x 820 y 310 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c1b "+ quality skimped" #b5482e x 820 y 260 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c2 "fixed prices:\nshortage" #b5482e cites ["kornai1980"] x 820 y 415 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c3 "returns to\ncapital fall" #b5482e cites ["easterlyfischer1995"] x 820 y 300 font_size 32 hidden true
    text c4 "Allen:\nplanners' mistakes" #8a5fa8 cites ["allen2003"] x 820 y 190 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Where it stalled"

A plan is good at big, obvious things. So what happened once the obvious things were built?
    draw axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

B: Here's the slowdown: about three and a half percent a year in the fifties an (@draw bar_4 bar_5@)d sixties, under one and a half in the seventies and eighties.

Look inside one factory. Say Harbourtown's boatyard runs under a plan, with illustrative numbers: a target of twenty boats, though it could build thirty.
    draw boat by tg cb

    quiz "You run the boatyard. Next year's target will be this year's output, plus a bit. How many boats do you build?" judge false voice b
        right: Many managers aimed just over the target, and kept the rest quiet.
        * 20
        * 22
        * 30

Beat the target by a lot, and next year's plan demands the lot. Economists c (@draw c1@)all it the ratchet: so managers hid what they could really do.

B: And whatever the target doesn't count, (@draw c1b@) like quality or what buyers actually want, gets skimped.

    erase boat by tg cb

    move c1 c1b by [0,250] duration 0.8

Meanwhile prices, fixed by planners, couldn' (@draw c2@)t say what was scarce: hence the queues and bare shelves of the shortage economy János Kornai described.

B: But those faults were there in the fifties, when growth was fast. Why did it slow only later?
    highlight bar_2 bar_3

William Easterly and Stanley Fischer's answer: Soviet growth came  (@draw c3@)mostly from piling up machines, and each extra machine added less.

B: Market economies pile up machines too.
    highlight bar_4 bar_5

They do, but they keep finding good uses for the extra machines. The Soviet economy was unusually bad at that, so its returns fell faster.
    highlight c3

Robert Allen, though, (@draw c4@) blames choices more than the system: huge arms spending and wasteful investment that a better plan could have avoided.

    pause 0.5

## What it did well
