    card title "What it did well"

So the incentives were poor, and the easy growth ran out. Is that the whole verdict?
    draw axes

B: Not even close. Take life expectancy in Russia: in 1928, thirty-nine years. America's was fifty-eight.
    draw line_1 line_2 t28 parallel true

By 1960, six (@draw t60@)ty-eight against seventy. Almost the whole gap closed in a generation, helped by medicine the whole world got.

B: Schooling too. In 1897  (@draw lit@)about a quarter of the people could read, by 1926 about half, by the 1950s nearly everyone.

And in 1957 the first sa (@draw sput@)tellite in orbit, Sputnik, was Soviet. Aim a whole country at a few goals, and it can hit them.

    erase lit sput t28

B: Then the same stall: Russian life expectancy stuck near sixty-eight for twenty-five years, while America's kept rising.
    animate stage 1 duration 3

    draw t85

And the costs were enormous. Forced collectivisati (@draw fam@)on of the farms was a major cause of the famine of (@point at.data [1932,32.8] gesture circle@) 1930 to 1933, which killed an estimated 5.7 to 8.7 million people.

B: And did it take a plan at all? Japan was about as (@draw jap@) poor in 1913, and by 1975 it had reached seventy percent of America's income, without one.

    erase fam jap

How sure are we? That the plan b (@draw s1@)uilt industry, schools and health fast, at a terrible human cost: consensus.

B: Why it stalled, and (@draw s2@) whether markets could have done as well with less suffering: still contested.

So planning could hit a few huge targets, and kept missing the millions of small ones that keep an economy growing.
    highlight line_1

    quiz "A government wants every village on the electricity grid within ten years. Where would a central plan do best?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Building the grid: one big, visible target a plan can push through.
        wrong: Which of these is one big, obvious target, and which are millions of small choices? Here, building the grid.
        + Building the grid everywhere at once
        * Deciding which appliances each family buys
        * Setting the price of every appliance

    quiz "A health ministry judges hospitals by the number of operations they do. What should it expect?" voice b
        right: More quick operations: what the target counts goes up, and what it leaves out, like quality, slips.
        wrong: Ask what the count leaves out: here, more quick, simple operations, with quality slipping.
        * Better operations, same number
        + More quick, simple operations, with quality slipping
        * No change in what doctors do

    quiz "A planner fixes the price of meat at half what shoppers would pay, and plans the same output as last year. What will shoppers see?"
        right: Queues and empty counters: at the low fixed price shoppers want more than the plan makes, and no price rises to tell anyone.
        wrong: At a price far below what buyers would pay, compare how much they want with how much is made: here, queues and empty counters.
        + Queues and empty counters
        * Meat piling up unsold
        * Just enough meat for everyone

B: Which raises the obvious question: does it have to be one or the other?

    pause 0.5

